	Wanderley started performing and recording professionally around 18. He recorded over 18 albums in Brazil. He gained notoriety during the 1960s with his band, Walter Wanderley e Seu Conjunto. He accompanied João Gilberto for whom he did arrangements for Gilberto''s third album. Married to singer Isaura Garcia, Wanderley moved to the USA in 1965, invited by [a=Creed Taylor] to sign with [l=Verve Records] after [a=Tony Bennett] recommended him to Taylor. Walter''s first album for Verve, with [a=José Marino] on bass and drummer [a=Claudio Slon] plus guests [a=Bucky Pizzarelli] and [a=Urbie Green], "Rain Forest," included the Billboard Top 50 hit "So Nice" (Summer Samba). After several albums for Verve (featuring such musicians as [a=Dom Um Romao], [a=Paulinho Magalhaes] and [a=Marcos Valle], he signed to A&M/CTI, recording albums orchestrated by [a=Don Sebesky] and [a=Eumir Deodato], featuring [a=João Palma], [a=Airto Moreira], [a=Hubert Laws] and [a=Marvin Stamm]. He later settled in California until he passed away after a long battle with bone cancer.

