	Joey left high school and attended the School Of Audio Engineering (SAE) in London from 1988 to 1989. Whilst studying he was DJ''ing at various bars and clubs as well as entering the DMC Mixing Championships. Using free studio time given at SAE Joey starting producing tracks, one of which was signed to Cooltempo, EMI by Simon Dunmore under the guise [a=Taste Of Paradise]. Following this Joey began releasing tracks under various pseudonyms, one of which being the infamous Cover Ups EPs. A string of remixes ensued while at the same time starting his label Ulterior Records. Joey launched Refunkt Records in 2002.');

	Born: 1940. 

	Died: November 1996 ');



	He was known primarily for his production and songwriting work on the New York-based labels [l=P&P Records], [l=Red Greg Records], [l=Salsoul Records], [l=Prelude Records] and major labels like [l=Atlantic], as well as his studio projects [a=Cloud One], [a=Bumblebee Unlimited], [a=Inner Life], [a=Musique], [a=Phreek] and [a=The Universal Robot Band]. He has won the ASCAP Songwriter of the Year Award 3 times and has earned 32 gold and platinum records.



	Patrick''s childhood was spent singing in choirs and attending concerts at the legendary Apollo Theater. His father bought him a trumpet and in his teens Adams began playing guitar and writing songs. He honed his arranging skills by dissecting the song structure and arrangement patterns of the records he heard on the radio.



	In 1970 he was appointed Vice President of A&R for [l=Perception Records (5)]/[l=Today Records] where he discovered teenage vocal trio [a=Black Ivory] and met his future friend and longtime musical collaborator [a=Leroy Burgess].



	In 1974 Adams left [l=Perception Records (5)]/[l=Today Records] and started his own production company, [l=Patrick Adams Productions, Inc.] He met [a=Peter Brown] of [l=P&P Records] through mutual friend [a=Lonnie Johnson] and started producing experimental records for Peter''s many labels. He also formed a musical collective called the [a=The P.A. System]. Some of the artists he has worked with include [a=Ace Spectrum], [a=Black Ivory], [a=Bruni Pagan], [a=Candi Staton], [a=Debbie Taylor], [a=Eddie Kendricks], [a=Fonda Rae], [a=Gladys Knight], [a=Herbie Mann], [a=Loleatta Holloway], [a=Main Ingredient], [a=Narada Michael Walden], [a=Rick James], [a=The Salsoul Orchestra], [a=Shannon], [a=Sister Sledge], [a=Skipworth & Turner], [a=The Spinners] and more.



	In the ''80s he started working as an engineer at [l=Power Play Studios] and did work for artists like [a=Eric B. & Rakim], [a=Heavy D. & The Boyz], [a=Keith Sweat], [a=R. Kelly], [a=Salt-N-Pepa] and [a=Teddy Riley].



	Although he is credited on hundreds of releases, the Atlantic Records disco project "Patrick Adams presents [a55592]" is one of the few times his name appears on the front of an album cover.');

	She began her professional singing career in 1969 in California as a cast member in the Los Angeles production of the musical Hair. She then joined the group Pollution, led by Dobie Gray (who also was appearing in Hair) and then became a member of the group Earthquire, who released an album in 1973 on Motown''s Natural Resources label.



	With Motown, Táta released four solo albums on the Tamla label: Full Speed Ahead (1976), Totally Táta (1977), Try My Love (1978) and Givin'' All My Love (1981). These albums feature a diverse range of genres including classic Motown soul, country or southern soul, disco, jazz, funk, country, Latin, doo-wop and gospel.



	She has had an active career as a back-up singer and soloist, working with Stevie Wonder, Andraé Crouch, Lou Rawls, Chaka Kahn, Patti LaBelle, Michael Jackson and Madonna. She also worked in film, performing the voice of Shug Avery in The Color Purple and is featured on four songs on the 1986 soundtrack album, one of which, Miss Celie''s Blues (Sister) was nominated for an Academy Award in the Best Song category.



	In 1998 she signed with Quincy Jones’s Quest Records and released a gospel album, Now I See. Vega is also known for her song SEASONS, which is a cover of a song by Japanese superstar Ayumi Hamasaki.

