	Connors was born and raised in Philadelphia, having an interest in jazz from an early age when he began playing drums. At elementary school he was exposed to jazz extensively, becoming heavily influenced by drummer [a=Lex Humphries] – the younger brother of bassist and [url=https://www.discogs.com/artist/262128-Art-Blakey-The-Jazz-Messengers]Jazz-Messenger[/url] player, [a=Spanky DeBrest]. Once sitting-in for [a=Elvin Jones] at a [a=John Coltrane] performance he attended while in middle school, Connors went on to study music at Temple University and Juilliard. 



	His first recording was for [a=Archie Shepp]''s "[m=128821]" (1967), then continuing by playing with [a=Pharoah Sanders] for the next few years, before signing to [l=Buddah Records] sublabel [l=Cobblestone], where he recorded his first release as a bandleader with album "[m=131721]" (1972). Connors began to focus more on R''n''B material in the mid-1970s after signing with Buddah Records, later becoming the label''s A&R manager. 



	He went on to lead jazz recordings with [a=Carlos Garnett], [a=Gary Bartz], [a=Dee Dee Bridgewater], and [a=Herbie Hancock]. In the mid-1970s, when turning to sophisticated R''n''B and soul material, he recorded work featuring guest singers such as [a=Michael Henderson], [a=Jean Carn], [a=Phyllis Hyman], [a=Al Johnson], [a=Jones Girls], among others, with his work becoming a main staple of jazz-fusion and jazz dance followers, with tracks including "[r=5626327]" from album "[m=570624]" (1974), "[url=https://www.discogs.com/master/640196-Norman-Connors-This-Is-Your-Life-Captain-Connors]Captain Connors[/url]" from album "[m=110483]" (1977), et al. 

