	Fonce Mizell also contributed to productions for [a=The Blackbyrds], [a=Bobby Humphrey], [a=Gary Bartz], [a=Johnny Hammond], [a=L.T.D.] and [a=A Taste Of Honey]. His work often focused on vocal arrangement, rhythmic development and overall song structure.

	Recordings produced by the Mizell Brothers have been reused and sampled in multiple later music genres, including hip-hop, dance music and modern soul.');



	Mizell began his professional career working within [l=Motown]’s songwriting and production environment, contributing to recordings by artists including [a=The Jackson 5]. During this period, he developed experience in arrangement, composition and studio production.

	In the early 1970s, Larry Mizell co-founded [l=Sky High Productions] with his brother, forming a long-term creative partnership. The Mizell Brothers became associated with [l=Blue Note] Records through their work with trumpeter [a=Donald Byrd]. Their collaborations resulted in a series of albums including Black Byrd (1973), Street Lady (1973), Stepping Into Tomorrow (1974) and Places And Spaces (1975), which combined elements of jazz, funk, soul and contemporary studio production techniques.



	Outside of his work with Byrd, Mizell contributed to recordings by artists including [a=The Blackbyrds], [a=Bobby Humphrey], [a=Gary Bartz], [a=Johnny Hammond], [a=A Taste Of Honey] and [a=L.T.D.]. His production work frequently incorporated layered arrangements, electric keyboards, synthesizers and structured vocal harmonies.

