	Marchetti first engaged in music by the mid-20s, after attending [a=Bruno Maderna]''s 1956 composition masterclasses in Milan, where Walter befriended Spanish composer [a=Juan Hidalgo], his life-long collaborator and creative partner. In 1957, Walter Marchetti visited [url=https://discogs.com/label/293404]Darmstadt International Summer Courses for New Music[/url], a hotbed of [i]serial music[/i] at the time. However, he soon grew disillusioned and frustrated with the formalistic approach of dodecaphony and serialism.



	At this turning point in his career, Walter Marchetti met [a=John Cage]; he was instantly captivated by Cage''s [i]aleatoric music[/i] endeavors with [a=David Tudor] and his reliance on silence as the core structural element. In January 1959, Marchetti participated in one of the country''s first international concerts of "random music," [i][url=https://discogs.com/release/754329]Noises at Rotonda[/url][/i], alongside John Cage, [a=Morton Feldman], and Juan Hidalgo.



	In 1960, Marchetti relocated to Spain and joined [i]Música Abierta[/i] ("Open Music") society in Barcelona, recently established by Juan Hidalgo with fellow composers [a=Josep Maria Mestres Quadreny], [a=Joaquim Homs], [a=Josep Cercós], and [a=Luis de Pablo]. By 1964, Walter moved to Madrid to start a more radical performance group, [b]zaj[/b], heavily inspired by [url=https://discogs.com/artist/1777058]Fluxus[/url]. Marchetti and Hidalgo worked with [a=Ramón Barce], [a=José Luis Castillejo], and other contemporary Spanish thinkers and artists to organize concerts, exhibitions, and installations. ZAJ members participated in several [a=George Maciunas] events and collaborated with other [i]Fluxus[/i] associates and scholars over the years, including [a=Demetrio Stratos] and [a=Gianni-Emilio Simonetti].



	By the mid-1970s, after his USA tour with [b]zaj[/b] organized by [url=https://discogs.com/artist/453529]Mimi Johnson[/url], Marchetti returned to Italy and settled in Milan. In June 1978, he participated in the musical happening [i][url=https://discogs.com/release/2503683]Il treno di John Cage[/url][/i] and discussed it with the composer and [a=Tito Gotti] on [l=RAI Radiotelevisione Italiana] radio. Walter remained active until at least the mid-2000s — composing, recording, and performing at prominent venues across Italy, including [l=Teatro Olimpico di Vicenza], and abroad in France, Germany, the United States, and Japan. He died of a heart attack at 84.');

	From 1974 to 1999 Santana performed with a host of renowned musicians, regularly releasing albums over the years. Under his own label, Guts And Grace, he has released the collaborative album Brothers (1994). In June 1999 he released [m77696], the biggest hit of his career, winning 8 Grammy Awards. In 2003 Rolling Stone magazine listed Carlos Santana at number 20 on their list of the 100 Greatest Guitarists of All Time. In 2013 Carlos Santana earned the distinction of becoming a Kennedy Center honoree for his contribution to the arts.

	In 2016 Carlos Santana teamed up with 2 of the greatest living jazz musicians, saxophonist [a29979] and keyboardist [a3865], in a new jazz-rock collective called Mega Nova. The group also features respected jazz bassist [a79989] and Carlos'' wife and current Santana drummer,  [a268298]-Santana.');

