	Tom is currently playing in the hit Broadway show The Drowsy Chaperone (starring Sutton Foster) and celebrating the release of his new CD, "Cole Porter Moods". This CD is the first in a series called the "American Songbook for the Modern Ear". As Murray explains, "By resetting Cole Porter''s masterpieces with contemporary and world flavor, they become accessible to today''s listener." Eleven songs are sung and played on acoustic and electric instruments in styles ranging from flamenco and Cuban to techno and acid jazz.

	Murray is originally from Brockton, Massachusetts. He attended the Hartt School of Music, where he studied saxophone, clarinet, flute, and jazz with legends Jackie McLean, Walter Bishop Jr., and Jaki Byard. In 1984 he moved to New York City and has been freelancing all over the world ever since.');



	British electronic music composer, improviser and instrument builder (23 April 1943, Exmouth, Devon — 1 January 2005, London). [b]Hugh Davies[/b] was the first UK composer to perform "live electronic music," renowned for making unique DIY electroacoustic instruments (held in the [url=https://discogs.com/label/1441629]Science Museum[/url]''s collection in London). As a musicologist and archivist, he created one of the most comprehensive compendiums of early electronic music, a monumental [i]International Electronic Music Catalog[/i] (Répertoire International Des Musiques Electroacoustiques) co-authored with [a1770847] at [l=INA-GRM] in France and published by [l=The MIT Press] in 1967. Davies worked at Electronic Music Workshop (EMW) at [l=Goldsmiths College] from 1968 to 1986, one of England''s earliest academic electronic music studios. Since 1999 and till his death, he was a part-time researcher and lecturer at [l=Middlesex University]''s Centre for Electronic Arts in London.



	Davies studied music at [l=Worcester College, Oxford], between 1961 and ''64, with future members of [url=https://discogs.com/artist/262731]Monty Python''s Flying Circus[/url] as his classmates. Hugh was indoctrinated into electronic music by [a=Daphne Oram] of [a=BBC Radiophonic Workshop], producing his first-ever electronic composition at Oram''s [l=Tower Folly] studio in London. From 1964 to ''66, Hugh Davies was an assistant to [a=Karlheinz Stockhausen] in Cologne, Germany, experiencing a new form of "live" electronica, made with real-time processing rather than pre-assembled on magnetic tape. Davies appeared with Stockhausen''s ensemble in several concerts, including the ''67 [i][r=2117497][/i] LP released by [l=CBS] (re-issued in 1995 by [l=Stockhausen-Verlag] as [url=https://discogs.com/release/1162457]Vol 9[/url] of the composer''s complete edition).



	After returning to England in 1967, Hugh Davies built his first DIY instruments with contact microphones on various household items, from an egg slicer to fretsaw blades and furniture wheels. His best-known creation was "[i]Shozyg I/II[/i]" — an assemblage of amplified tiny found objects inside the hollow encyclopedia''s tome cover (named after the SHO–ZYG alphabetic range on the spine). Davis composed several pieces for "Shozygs," the first devices he explicitly referred to as "instruments for live electronic performance." In 1969, he made ten hand-numbered "Shozygs" for [i][r=1185972][/i], an issue of [a=Henri Chopin]''s avantgarde art magazine. (One of the copies is in [url=https://discogs.com/label/72301]MoMA[/url]''s permanent collection in New York.) Since the 1970s, Davis began working with suspended and reverberating metal, building the "[i]Springboard[/i]" series, arrays of long springs stretched on woodblocks and amplified by electromagnetic pickups.



	Davies performed in numerous ensembles, including [b][a=Gentle Fire][/b] (1968–75), established by his friend [url=https://discogs.com/artist/580364]Richard Orton[/url] at the [l=University Of York], and [b][a=The Music Improvisation Company][/b] (69–71) that Hugh co-founded with free jazz legends [a=Derek Bailey], [a=Evan Parker] and [a=Jamie Muir]. He also played with [b]Naked Software[/b] (1968–73) and [b]Artist Placement Group[/b] (APG) in the mid-70s (alongside [a=Ian Breakwell], [a=John Latham] and several other notable British conceptual artists). Hugh participated in Derek Bailey''s [b][url=https://discogs.com/artist/326370]Company[/url][/b] improvisations in May 1983, playing in several [url=https://discogs.com/release/1786668]trios[/url] with [a=Joëlle Léandre], [a=Vinko Globokar] and [a=Evan Parker]. (Previously unreleased [l=BBC] radio broadcasts from the same era later appeared on [i][r=14792427][/i] 2xLP from [l=Honest Jon''s Records] in 2020.) Hugh Davies recorded with [a=Alan Tomlinson], [a=Phil Minton] and [a=Roger Turner] as [b][a=The Ferals][/b] on [l=Leo Records] in 1986 and appeared on the ''88 [i][m=26436][/i] album by [a=Talk Talk]. Other musicians and instrument builders that he collaborated with include [a=Lily Greenham], [a=Peter Cusack], [a=John Russell], [a=Hilary Jeffery], [a=Hans-Karsten Raecke], [a=Max Eastley], [a=David Toop], and [a=Paul Burwell].');





