	Classically trained and educated, Horn was an active member in the 1950`s west coast jazz scene, recording with Chico Hamilton, Shorty Rogers and Buddy Collette among others. In the late 1950`s and early 1960`s with his own group, he took up a more modal approach to playing, influenced by Miles Davis. In addition to a large discography of jazz recordings, Horn is considered one of the pioneers of new age music, in which the flute became his main instrument.



	His 1968 album of solo flute and vocals (Inside, also known as Inside The Taj Mahal) has sold over three quarters of a million copies and launched a series of Inside records recorded in natural and sacred sites with unique acoustic properties. Both Inside and Inside II were top 10 albums on the Billboard jazz chart. Horn won two Grammy Awards for his 1965 album Jazz Suite On The Mass Texts. Other Grammy nominated albums include Cycle (1965), Traveler (1987), and Inside Monument Valley (1999). In 1990 he published his autobiography Inside Paul Horn (The Spiritual Odyssey Of A Universal Traveler), co-authored by [a315650]. He is the father of [a=Robin Horn].');

	Began career at KLIF radio, in Dallas, Texas. Relocated to Los Angeles, and worked packaging records for for George Jay. Then after further radio roles in Wichita Falls, Texas, returned to Los Angeles, and joined Liberty in promotions in 1959, soon becoming a producer, scoring numerous early 1960s hits by white rock-n-roll artists. He was a staff producer at Liberty Records from 1959 to 1965, and was responsible for hiring [a=Phil Spector] as a producer for Liberty, and discovered [a=Bobby Vee]. And was head of A&R from 1961. In 1965, he formed production company [l=Snuff Garrett Productions]. In 1966, he started his own record label, Snuff Garrett Records, and founded [l=Viva (3)] Records. In 1968, he founded [l=Amigo Studios], in North Hollywood, California, purchased from Steve Douglas for $70,000, and set up [a=J.J. Cale] as recording engineer. In late 1968, he sold his companies to Warner Bros. for $2.25 million, and intended to retire to Dallas. After a year, he returned to Los Angeles and restarted his career from scratch. In 1970, he formed [l=Garrett Music Enterprises], and was producing for Kapp and MCA, scoring hits for Cher, then later in the 1970s, producing country music, including reacquiring the Viva label, with Clint Eastwood, and founded [l=The Nostalgia Merchant]. Retired from the music business in 1983 to Arizona after suffering a stroke.');

