	Began career as a pre-teen at Fine Recording Studios. On March 1, 1975, he became a staff mixing engineer at Mediasound. In 1976, was involved in the creation and building of the Sigma Sound Studios, New York, branch of the Philadelphia-based recording studio, and was chief engineer. Left the studio in 1978 to complete work on his invention, the Sontec Compudisk disk mastering computer. Returned to Sigma Sound in 1980. Then invented the industry-standard Lynx synchronizer circa 1985, and founded the company TimeLine in 1985. Left the music industry in 1995 to pursue a career in avionics, and was president and CEO of Sandel Avionics.
