	He began arranging professionally in his teens for Nat Towles. Played trumpet in the early ''40s with [a=Bob Astor], [a=Les Lieber], [a=Charlie Barnet], [a=Bobby Byrne], [a=Charlie Spivak], and [a=Horace Heidt]. He joined up with [a=Woody Herman] in 1944 and gained a strong reputation for composing (e.g. "The Good Earth" and "Wild Root") and arranging (e.g. "Woodchopper''s Ball" and "Blowin'' Up a Storm") while still playing trumpet.

	From 1950 he wrote dozens of songs for [a=Count Basie] including "Li''l Darlin''", "Cute", "Whirly Bird", "Little Pony" and "Kid from Red Bank". He also led his own band intermittently for a few years in the ''50s but from the ''60s concentrated on writing TV and film scores, e.g. themes from Batman, Barefoot in the Park, and The Odd Couple.

