	When producer [a97480] launched [l11717] as an independent entity in February 1970, he brought Chirumbolo on as vice president in charge of sales and marketing. While many legacy jazz labels like [l281], [l19591] and Taylor’s own [l26557] were failing to find an audience, Chirumbolo’s efforts helped make CTI the single most successful jazz label of the seventies. 



	By 1973, Chirumbolo left CTI to start the short-lived [l170654] and form [l1441935], which handled productions for [a2763444]’s [l77694] label. Between 1977 and 1980, Chirumbolo produced about a dozen records for Versatile (and later Buddah) by such artists as [a29956], [a211619], [a268216] and [a34183] and helped license about twenty additional recordings by [a136133], [a10584], [a160905] and others.

	 

	Chirumbolo’s productions for Versatile were styled very much like the early CTI records with gatefold sleeves, striking cover art and large casts of New York City session musicians, often arranged by [a829125]. Indeed, Chirumbolo recorded albums by former CTI artists [a12633] and [a22855] and Versatile’s earliest releases even utilized CTI’s 6000 series numbering. Unfortunately, many of the well-produced jazz sessions were mired by then fashionable disco treatments and while the albums never found much of an audience, they have since become cult collector’s items. 



	Returning to CTI briefly between 1981 and 1983, Chirumbolo passed away on October 24, 1987, at age 58.

