	Hall combined musical training with studies in theater and electronics to become an audio engineer. He got his start in the business at Glendale''s Whitney Studios, where he worked as a second engineer on sessions for [a=Barry White], [a=Rod Stewart] and [a=Phil Spector]. After [l878] bought the studios he was soon drawn to mastering where his talent was quickly recognized. Among Steve’s earliest mastering projects were [a=Nick Gilder]’s "Hot Child In The City", [a=Blondie]’s "Heart of Glass", [a=Pat Benatar]’s "In the Heat of the Night" and [a=The Knack (3)]"My Sharona."



	In October 1982 Steve and business partner Gary Rice struck out on their own, envisioning a mastering facility focused on the cutting edge. That dream was realized with Future Disc, which grew rapidly from a one room suite to a multi-room complex encompassing 6000 square feet. Steve has always believed in the advancement of technology in the industry being one of the first to embrace CD mastering in 1982. At Future Disc, Steve has amassed a discography of recordings including Madonna’s "True Blue", Bobby Brown’s "Don''t Be Cruel", the soundtrack for "City of Angels," Green Day’s "Nimrod", Jane’s Addiction’s "Nothing’s Shocking", Toy Matinee’s "Toy Matinee", and Alice In Chain’s “Dirt” among other studio projects.



	As a sonic pioneer, Future Disc was one of the first mastering facilities to embrace surround sound technology and one of the first to set up for DVD authoring. With his work in the surround field, Hall has mastered surround projects such as The Band’s "The Last Waltz", Earth Wind & Fire’s "Gratitude", the Grateful Dead’s "American Beauty"/"Workingman’s Dead", Jackson Browne''s "Runnin’ On Empty", Foreigner IV, The Beach Boys’ "Pet Sounds" and "Earth Wind & Fire And Chicago Live At The Greek",



	Recent mastering projects that Steve has completed include American Idol 2008, Chick Corea, The Rippingtons, Rick Braun & Richard Elliot (“RnR”), Blind Melon, The Traveling Wiburys'' “Volumes 1 & 3," CD/surround mixes for Don Grusin’s session "The Hang", the Blasters’ "Going Home", Last of The Breed''s “Live In Concert” and for the Surround Music Award-tribute to George Harrison titled “The Concert for George”.



