	Aged 10 he discovered jazz while living in a still Nazi occupied Italy, and after WWII, black GIs gave him his first jazz "education". Since his mother was a hat designer from Monte Carlo (and her father had worked in the casinos), he learned English from her and became an anglophile. In the late 50s he felt the urge to become a filmmaker in order to document the birth of the British jazz scene, and he moved to the UK. Soon he came in contact with people like [a270085], already a star, [a291339], [a1053895], [a270088], [a262455] and the fledgling [a20991]. In 1978, he relocated to New York and tried to open up a market for European, progressive rock acts that he''d been "dealing" with, like [a10346], [a718671], [a152680] and [a179749]. From then on, until the 2010''s, he ran the rehearsal and recording studio [b]Blue Door[/b] near Chelsea. He died of cancer 13 January 2016, in NYC, New York, USA.');



