	From 1956 onward, Pomus (lyrics) and Mort Shuman (music) formed one of the most successful Brill Building–era teams, writing bluesy, teen-oriented songs that dominated Atlantic Records and beyond. Their collaboration produced dozens of chart hits and is credited with shaping the sound and style of early 1960s rock and pop.

	Pomus credits include over 1,000 songs and about 561 registered copyrights; estimates suggest sales of around 100 million recordings featuring his work. He was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame in 1992 (Non-Performer, posthumously) and received multiple Grammy Awards. He was awarded the Pioneer Award by the Rhythm and Blues Foundation and was a founder and trustee of that organization, helping support overlooked R&B artists. In 2015, Rolling Stone ranked Pomus and Shuman together as No. 61 on its list of the 100 greatest songwriters of all time. Husband of [a=Willi Burke].');

	Named Peter Robinson but later adopted by Muriel and Jack Spedding, and renamed Christopher John Spedding.



