	Prominent drummers such as [a=Buddy Rich], [a=Gene Krupa], & [a=George Wettling] hailed Chick Webb as the best and most influencial of all the Big Band drummers. He was born with a physical deformity described as TB of the spine. To earn money for his first set of drums at age 11 he worked as a newsboy. By the early 1920s he was playing drums on excursion boats in Baltimore Harbor. He moved to New York where he freelanced. He played with and became lifelong friends with [a=Duke Ellington] . Webb formed his first small band in 1926. Slowly gaining momentum, in 1935 he hired the shy and unsure teen-aged [a=Ella Fitzgerald]. Now headlining at The Savoy Ballroom in Harlem, where his driving style of drumming lit up the ballroom making it the hottest dance floor in the country, Chick Webb`s band became as prominent as the likes of Ellington, Fletcher Henderson, and Cab Calloway.  In 1938. now with [a=Van Alexander] as his primary arranger, Webb and Ella became national stars with the million seller "A-Tisket, A-Tasket". His final recording was on April 21, 1939.

	 Also known as Little Chick, Webb had suffered from ill health from birth. His spine was malformed. Peers note that he rarely complained, was "an uncommonly nice man to work with", he "hardly ever became upset when things went wrong", & that he was "very unselfish". Ella Fitzgerald''s legal guardian, Chick Webb passed away June 16, 1939 at John Hopkins Hospital in Baltimore with his wife Sally Hart Webb at his side.');

	Born in Rochester, New York, Famoudou performed in a church choir and various drum and bugle corps (marching ensembles) during his youth. He was exposed to jazz from an early age – his mother worked for a local social club, located door-to-door with a jazz venue that hosted musicians like [a=Kenny Burrell] and [a=Jimmy McGriff], and often took her son to concerts, including [a=Mahalia Jackson] performances. Don Moye''s uncles played saxophone, while his father was a drummer.

	Moye studied percussion at Wayne State University in Detroit. A trumpeter [a=Charles Moore], who happened to live in the same building, became Famoudou''s mentor and introduced him to the Association for the Advancement of Creative Musicians (AACM). In 1968, Famoudou Don Moye travelled to Europe with his first band, [b]Detroit Free Jazz[/b]. He eventually spent a few years abroad, working for RAI (Italian Radio and Television) briefly, and collaborating extensively with Moroccan musicians, as well as many notable jazz figures, such as [a=Steve Lacy], [a=Pharoah Sanders], [a=Sonny Sharrock], [a=Randy Weston], and [a=Art Taylor].

	In 1969, while rehearsing and performing at the [l=American Center For Students And Artists] in Paris, Moye met saxophonist [a=Roscoe Mitchell], who was in Europe with his quartet The Art Ensemble of Chicago (also featuring [a=Joseph Jarman] (sax), [a=Lester Bowie] (trumpet) and [a=Malachi Favors] on bass). AEC already released three albums on [l=BYG Records] at the time, but four members were playing percussion parts themselves, and lacked a dedicated performer to complete a rhythm section. Roscoe Mitchell asked Don Moye to join the group.

	After returning to the United States in the early 1970s, Famoudou Don Moye had played with [a=Black Artists Group] in St. Louis for a while, and finally settled in Chicago. Apart from an extensive work the The Art Ensemble Of Chicago, Moye also played in a duo with percussionist [a=Steve McCall]. In 1984, the musician joined [a=The Leaders (3)] jazz supergroup. Famoudou Don Moye played and recorded with different bands and ensembles of [a=Don Pullen], [a=Cecil McBee], [a=Hamiet Bluiett], [a=Julius A Hemphill], [a=Chico Freeman] and [a=Lester Bowie''s Brass Fantasy].

