	A child prodigy, Sid Page started on the violin when he was still in kindergarten. Playing his violin he won contests and by the time he was 13 years of age he had played with the Santa Rosa Symphony.  He once had aspirations of becoming a teacher but after a year-and-a-half at college, he decided it wasn''t for him. In 1969, Page was working as a shoe salesman when he met [a431746] in a Santa Rosa bar. He was a good salesman and his employer was sad to see him go when he left to join [a496322]. He said he could have his job back if things didn''t work out.



	By 1974, Page along with drummer [a397977] were the new faces in [a39768]. His violin is heard throughout the album and is noted in Head Heritage''s "Unsung" article about [i][m=78530][/i] as adding [i]" an extra dimension of sweetness and warmth to the proceedings."[/i] As a member of the band, Page appeared with them on [i]Soul Train[/i] performing "If You Want Me to Stay" and "Stand", The Mike Douglas Show in 7/74 performing "I Want to Take You Higher". One one [i]Soul Train[/i] appearance, Page is playing his violin through a wha-wha device in the jam leading up to "Thank You (Falettinme Be Mice Elf Again)"



	He played fiddle on the track "Superkicker" which appears on the 1978 album  [i][m=605595][/i] album by  [a1693405]. He is credited in the strings section on the [i][m=431051][/i] album by [a942225] which was released in 1979 as well as [i][r=2778747][/i] by [a219496] and many more. 



	In addition to performing on film sound tracks, he is also a concert master.



	Page and [a2562357] had a release [i][r=7248157][/i] in 1984. In 2002, his album he did with Guy Maeda,  [i][r=14933479][/i] was released.');

