	He spent his formative years in Blackpool where he played with various local bands and formed [a=The Executives (2)]. Early in 1965, he was invited to join the Liverpool group [a=The Remo Four] as organist and vocalist. The group spent some time being the resident band at the [l=Star-Club Hamburg]; they followed this with a US tour accompanying [a=The Beatles]. At the end of the 1960s, he formed a new group with drummer [a=Roy Dyke] and bassist [a=Kim Gardner]. They called themselves [a=Ashton, Gardner & Dyke]. When the band broke up in 1973, he briefly joined [a=Family (6)]. He played on sessions with [a=Jerry Lee Lewis], [a=George Harrison], [a=Eric Clapton], [a=Paul McCartney], and [a=Jon Lord]. He passed through the ranks of [a=Chicken Shack], [a=John Entwistle''s Rigor Mortis], and [a=Medicine Head (2)]. In August 1976, when [a=Deep Purple] split, Jon Lord and [a=Ian Paice] combined with Ashton. The result was the formation of [a=Paice Ashton & Lord]. During the 1980s, he co-hosted a TV show with [a=Rick Wakeman]. By the early 1990s, he began to develop his second career as a painter. His work can be seen on the covers of various CDs. He died from cancer at his home.

