	Angélique Kidjo grew up in Ouidah (still known as the Slave Port) in Benin.
	 
	She was born into a musical family: her mother is a choreographer and director of a theater group, her brothers are instrumentalists, and at the age of six, she was singing and dancing in her mother''s company and later joined her brothers'' group, the Kidjo Brothers Band, where she sang a variety of Beninese songs. Growing up in Benin exposed her to a rich variety of music. She was heavily influenced by the traditional folk styles and love songs of the country—epic songs full of allusions to the history of the villages and the rituals and Vodou ceremonies of the older generation, but, perhaps more importantly, she was listening to the new urban African music percolating up from South Africa and the Western songs ranging from James Brown and [a=Aretha Franklin] to [a=Jimi Hendrix] that she heard on the radio.
	 
	The formidable South African vocalist [a=Miriam Makeba] was a great influence on the young Kidjo, so much so that Angelique, who was already making a name for herself, recorded an adaptation of a Makeba song for Beninese radio. Her first hits followed and she toured the Ivory Coast.

	It wasn''t until Kidjo made a trip to Europe that her career came alive. It was Cameroonian producer [b]Ekambi Brilliant[/b] who suggested that Angelique move to Paris to record and the singer left Benin for Europe. Paris in the 1980s was the breeding ground for the new African music and African artists, freed from the constraints of African tradition, yet incorporating their roots with Western styles that were generating revolutionary sounds.
	 
	When she first arrived, she sang and recorded with a group called [a=Alafia] and then joined the jazz-tinged band [b]Pili Pili[/b]. She later recorded two jazz albums with Pili Pili and played with it at the Montreux Jazz Festival in 1986. In the same year, she joined forces with bass player and composer [a=Jean Hebrail], and together they worked on their own music. Through regularly working at [l759399] in Paris, she also met many African and Antillean musicians and was able to form her own group and release her first solo album, “Parakou.”

	She won the 2023 Polar Music Prize (as announced on March 28).');

	Volquartz was raised in Duisburg-Homberg and learned to play guitar and saxophone as a schoolboy. He came in touch with the musicians, who formed "Ambition In Music", which eventually evolved to Annexus Quam in 1969. After they recorded their first album in 1970, the band tuned out to become more experimental. Volquartz was then also part of the line-up of the second album, after which the band disbanded.

