	Kreutzfeldt began his professional career in 1967 as additional guitarist with [a=Lollipops] (at the beginning behind the scenes). With [a=Steen Toft Andersen] he formed the duo [a=Stig & Steen] in the early 1970s and then became co-lead singer of [a=Ache (2)] from 1975 to 1976. In 1976 Kreutzfeldt was headhunted by [a=Freddy Hansson] for Hansson''s new [l=Sweet Silence Studios], where he helped build the studio from scratch. Until 1981 he worked there as a permanent freelance technician and producer.

	In 1977 he met [a=Jens Rugsted] in Sweet Silence. The first two [a=Rugsted/Kreutzfeldt] albums were released in 1979 and 1980, and a major national breakthrough came after a TV appearance in March 1981. Kreutzfeldt also served as A&R manager and producer for [l=CBS] and was appointed director of the Danish division in 1979, but he had to leave this position when Rugsted/Kreutzfeldt broke through in 1981.

	In the Sweet Silence era, Kreutzfeldt worked with international artists such as [a=Oscar Peterson], [a=Cat Stevens], [a=Ringo Starr] and Danish artists such as [a=C. V. Jørgensen] and [a=Bifrost]. Later he also produced for [a=Sebastian (5)], [a=Sanne Salomonsen], [a=Lis Sørensen], [a=Lex & Klatten] and [a=Brdr. Olsen], for whom he produced the winner of the year 2000 Eurovision Song Contest [m=207217].

