	Following the Stock Market Crash and the separation of his parents, Miles moved with his mother to Long Beach, California in 1929. After high school he joined the Navy and, following his discharge, moved to Los Angeles to enroll at Chouinard Art Institute.
	After working in New York in the early 1950s for [a1915387] and "Esquire" magazine, Miles was hired in his own right around 1955 by [a292019] of the jazz record label [l281] to design album covers when the label began releasing their recordings on 12" LPs. Miles designed several hundred covers, frequently incorporating Wolff''s session photographs and, later, his own photographs, although many of his later designs dispensed entirely with photographs.
	Miles wasn''t particularly interested in jazz, professing to have much more of an interest in classical music, but used the descriptions of the sessions relayed to him by producer [a252962] to create the artwork.
	Lion''s retirement as a record producer in 1967 coincided with the end of Miles'' connection with Blue Note. "Fifty Bucks an album...they loved it, thought it was modern, they thought it went with the music...one or two colors to work with at that time and some outrageous graphics!".');





