	American recording engineer (30 August 1923, Summerland, Canada — 15 December 2010, Oxnard, California). Hugh Davies was best known for working at [l=Capitol Records] from 1950 to 1985 and [l=Harmonia Mundi] (1985–90). He won two Grammy Awards for "Best Engineered Recording," for [i][m=464242][/i] in 1961 and [a=Glen Campbell]''s [i][m=152001][/i] in ''69. Davies grew up in British Columbia and served in the Royal Canadian Airforce during World War II. He relocated to the United States in 1950, settling in California and working for [l=Capitol Records, Inc.] for over 35 years. Hugh B. Davies recorded and engineered many renowned musicians, including [a=Bob Hope], [a=Frank Sinatra] and [a=Frank Sinatra Jr.], [a=Nelson Riddle], [a=Tennessee Ernie Ford], [a=Buck Owens], [a=Helen Reddy], [a=Bobby Darin], [a=Nat King Cole], [a=Gram Parsons], and [a=The Beach Boys]. In 1964, he was a live sound engineer for [a=The Beatles] at the [l=Hollywood Bowl].');



	He was known in the jazz community going back to the 1960s for the albums Strawberry Wine and Summer Night. He settled in Los Angeles and performed with Shorty Rogers, Teddy Edwards, Bud Shank, Red Norvo, Chet Baker, Joe Pass, Quincy Jones, Oliver Nelson, Shelly Manne, and Zoot Sims. In the 1970s Wofford toured Europe with Manne and Lee Konitz, in the 1980s Japan with Manne, Sweets Edison, and Eddie "Lockjaw" Davis, and Japan and Brazil with Benny Carter.



	Returning to San Diego, he performed with Kenny Burrell, Benny Golson, Art Farmer, Charlie Haden, Slide Hampton, Clifford Jordan, Ray Brown, and Charles McPherson. Wofford worked on John Lennon''s album Rock ''n'' Roll (1975) and in 1973 briefly toured with Roger McGuinn of the Byrds.



	On August 13, 2012, he was given the Lifetime Achievement Award at the 22nd Annual San Diego Music Awards.');



