	In 1980 she debuts with unrecorded band Sweet Intentions. Her breakthrough came in May 1983 she replaces [a=Gitte Naur] as singer in the pop-rock band [a=News]. Sings on the 1985 charity single [r=848374]. Duets with 1986-1989 husband [a=Thomas Helmig] for the hits [r=2349375] and the AIDS-awarenes single [r=1831784]. In January 1987 she leaves News to go on a US nuclear test protest tour called "Next Stop Nevada" together with [a=Moonjam]. Then joins brother [a=Lars Fenger] in the pop-rock/soul-funk band [a=Love Construction (2)] before pursuing a solo career. The first solo album [r=1293007] in 1989 had already sold in 70,000 copies by the end of the year, and 150,000 copies to date. After a long break and then a summer tour in 1991, Søs Fenger moved to Stockholm, Sweden with new husband, Swedish guitarist [a=Henrik Janson], and gave birth to their son [a=August Fenger Janson]. The Billie Holiday cover album [r=1579743] was released in 1992, which was positively received and had sold in 40,000 copies by autumn. There was a tour for the album in Sweden and Denmark. Søs Fenger moved back to Denmark in 1994 due to a divorce. The compilation album [r=1229213] was released in 1997, which is said to be her biggest commercial success. She has occasionally performed with Hounddog Allstars, an all-stars group with members from the Danish music scene.

