	Darrell began his recording career at [l269580] in 1966 while a film student at UCLA. He started mastering at [l373828] in 1967. Darrell and former Capitol Records A&R Executive Jack Chartoff launched [l1392527] in January of 1968. They built a multi-track recording studio and offices in Studio City on the site of Foto Kem, a major motion picture film laboratory, and production facility. Great Mandella established BMI and ASCAP affiliated publishing and signed several artists releasing singles, EP''s and LP''s on the Great Mandella, Quaint, Peace, and Gloryland Records labels. 



	Following Chartoff''s untimely death he returned to studio engineering and mastering at [l269580]. At Sunset in December of ''69, he mastered [m68202] for a 1970 release on [l1000] to be followed shortly by [r1644433] on [l5320] and then [r893988] on [l33520]. By mid-1970 he was under contract to [l5320] where he spent several years at [l314492]. While at MCA Darrell did extensive mastering work with MCA Decca''s famed country producer [a276524]. In addition to cutting many country hits, he also mastered pop material including first releases for [url=https://www.discogs.com/Elton-John-Elton-John/release/893988]Elton John[/url] and [url=https://www.discogs.com/Lynyrd-Skynyrd-Pronounced-L%C4%95h-n%C3%A9rd-Skin-n%C3%A9rd/release/2034523]Lynyrd Skynyrd[/url]. All of the early inscriptions bore his scribe "DJ" or "DWJ". 



	Darrell went on to become the first Mastering Engineer and Director of Engineering at [l280223]. It was JVC''s special half-speed mastering system developed to cut CD-4 Quad records and later modified for stereo audiophile half-speed disc mastering that leads him to use the "DWJ/2" inscription. In 1977 he founded [l267122] with [a211021] and [a254942]. Darrell is also a Commercial Pilot and was Vice President and principal partner in Southland Aviation a Southern California private aircraft charter operation for more than two decades. The advent of music video and stereo audio for television in the 1980''s took Darrell away from the recording studio to work in systems engineering for broadcasting where he remained until his retirement in 2010 to the Blue Ridge Mountains of Virginia, where he passed away on Jun 17, 2020 at the age of 71.



	Darrell''s Professional Affiliations include: The National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences (NARAS); Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers (SMPTE); Society of Broadcast Engineers (SBE); Audio Engineering Society (AES); Lifetime Fellow of the Hollywood Sapphire Group; Aircraft Owners and Pilots Association (AOPA); Baja Bush Pilots; Member of the Board of Directors of the John Edwards Memorial Foundation at UCLA.');

	 

