	Don was born into the family of Nina and William Simpson near Chicago. Don was the seventh of their eight children.

	 

	He shared the stage with many famous musicians, including [a847818], while Don was still in his teens. Don was rejected from service in WWII so he attended college instead. He supported himself by performing on radio and for dances writing his own arrangements for his own band. After graduation, he joined [a258689]''s band, writing and playing bass until being drafted into the Army in 1950 during the Korean War. At that time, his name was in lights under Gene Krupa''s at the Capitol Theatre in New York City!



	After scoring the highest in the Army Musicians Proficiency Assessment, Don became an instructor the day after Basic Training. He supervised performances, dances, and marched beside the 10th Division Band in parades on and off the Base at Fort Riley, KS.



	After the Army, Don joined [a263953]''s band, which was enjoying nationwide popularity.  Don enjoyed 59 years as a musician, principal arranger and composer with Ray Anthony. 



	In Hollywood, Don was in four movies with writing credits, recording sessions on CD''s, albums, singles, and live TV shows. He arranged the music for a [a439532] commercial using 80+ musicians! Don played and/or arranged the music and appeared in two weekly TV shows during the 1950''s and 60''s.



	Don spent two summers in New York City with Ray Anthony''s TV show and took classes at Columbia University in preparation for a teaching career. Don returned to USC to obtain his two Doctorate degrees: in Higher Education and in Musical Arts.



	Don began teaching in schools in Los Angeles, including Dorsey High School and Los Angeles City College.  He took students by bus to Las Vegas to perform in a parade; some had never been out of Los Angeles. They performed on TV there too!  He also took students to Catalina Island to perform in a parade and perform in the Pavillion during the evening as rewards for winning competitions. 

	His Dorsey High School band competed at the Hollywood Bowl for twelve years and won first prize for twelve years straight!



	A book was written years ago: "The Dark Tree" which was written about African-American musicians in L.A., and Don was mentioned several times regarding amusing incidents related to "Doc" (his nickname).



	He retired as Department Chair at Los Angeles City College, also having taught several jazz classes, composition, arranging, and concert bands while supervising approximately 35 or more part-time and full-time music teachers. Don held that position for many years.



	While in retirement in the Reno, NV area, Don still wrote for Ray Anthony. He also joined with musicians in the area providing dancing music for those still loved dancing to the big band sounds of yesteryear! The Sierra Swing Band continued for nearly twenty years with Don as the Music Director, arranger, bass player, while rehearsing weekly in an Assisted Living facility for their residents'' listening and dancing pleasure.');



