	Gerhardt grew up in Little Rock, Arkansas, where he began studying the piano at age five and composition at age nine. He studied music and engineering at several colleges and also studied piano privately Juilliard School. His formal education was interrupted by World War II. After the war, he enrolled at the universities of Illinois and Southern California. In 1950 he was hired by [l=RCA Victor] to transfer old 78 rpm recordings by artists like [a=Enrico Caruso] and [a=Artur Schnabel] in preparation for issue on the new Long Playing vinyl records. He then advanced to mixing and recording sessions by artists such as [a=Sir Adrian Boult], [a=Leopold Stokowski], [a=Massimo Freccia], [a=Alexander Gibson], [a=René Leibowitz], [a=Erich Leinsdorf], [a=Charles Munch], [a=Fritz Reiner], and [a=James Levine]. After working with [a=Arturo Toscanini] and the [a=NBC Symphony Orchestra], Toscanini encouraged him to study conducting. In 1955, Gerhardt left RCA Victor and began production work for [l=Westminster] records in London and Vienna. When the company went bankrupt, Gerhardt worked briefly for [l=Bell Sound Studios] producing the recordings of several pop music acts like [a=Eddie Fischer].



	In 1960, Gerhardt returned to [l=RCA Victor] and partnered with recording engineer [a=Kenneth Wilkinson] of [l=Decca] records (then [l=RCA]''s affiliate in Europe). Their first major project was a 12-LP set for [l=Reader''s Digest]: "[m=323115]." Their partnership lasted through 4,000 sessions. In 1961, Gerhardt sat in for a contracted conductor and led the [a=The Royal Philharmonic Orchestra] at a recording session in [l=Walthamstow Assembly Hall]. With the [l=Reader''s Digest] projects creating so much work, there was a need for another orchestra and conductor in London. By 1964, Gerhardt and [a=Sydney Sax] had formed an orchestra of top London orchestral and freelance musicians called the [a=National Philharmonic Orchestra]. Gerhardt created more than 600 albums with this group for Reader''s Digest over the next 12 years. [a=Leopold Stokowski] made some of his last recordings with this same orchestra. 



	His 1967 conducting of the so-called [a=RCA Symphony Orchestra] (which was the National Philharmonic Orchestra) of [a=Howard Hanson]''s "Symphony No. 2 (The Romantic)" garnered the praise of the composer. In 1968, Gerhardt recorded the first of his albums of film scores, entitled "[m=615432]." In 1972, due to the success of the film music arrangements, [l=RCA] contracted Gerhardt to produce a series of recordings, entitled Classic Film Scores. The first of these was the music for the film Sea Hawk by [a=Erich Wolfgang Korngold]. The composer''s son, [a=George Korngold], became the producer for the 12-album series, which included music by [a=Alfred Newman], [a=Max Steiner], [a=Franz Waxman], [a=Dimitri Tiomkin], [a=Bernard Herrmann], and [a=John Williams (4)]. Gerhardt used the National Philharmonic and prepared suites of the music when the original composer had not done so. Gerhardt would meet with the composers and discuss what form these arrangements should take, to remain faithful to their intentions. Many of Gerhardt''s suites continue to be used in concert performances. Mr. Gerhardt retired from RCA in 1986 and worked as a freelance producer until 1997.');

	While still working for Plastika, Rosenberg opens his own recording studio in 1955. Initially located at Borups Allé in Copenhagen, the studio moves to Islev Danseskole in 1957. Befriended with [a=Karl Emil Knudsen] from the late 50s and well into the 70s, Rosenberg does a lot of work for Knudsen''s [l=Storyville] label, including some fine blues recordings of artists like [a=Champion Jack Dupree], [a=Otis Spann], [a=Big Joe Williams], [a=Lonnie Johnson (2)], and [a=Sonny Boy Williamson (2)].

	In 1965 Rosenberg relocates his studio to Vanløse Bio (another movie theatre) where it stays until 1971. During this period it is known as [l=Ivar Rosenberg Lydteknik] and houses an advanced home-made 8-track recording machine. Here Rosenberg records many of the Danish progressive rock acts of the era, including [a=Beefeaters (2)], [a=Burnin Red Ivanhoe] and [a=Day Of Phoenix], and the studio becomes the master class for up-and-coming young engineers [a=Freddy Hansson] and [a=Flemming Rasmussen]. 

	In 1971 Rosenberg enters a closer relation with the [l=Sonet / Dansk Grammofon A/S] record company. His studio moves to the same building on Dortheavej 39 (Copenhagen) that houses Sonet and is renamed to [l=Rosenberg Studio]. Karl Emil Knudsen and Sonet takes the majority share when they pay for upgrading the studio with a 24-track tape recorder and an advanced mixing board. In the 1970s the Rosenberg Studio is at it''s peak as the most prominent recording studio in Denmark. It''s here that [a=Roy Thomas Baker] produces [a=Gasolin'']s milestone fifth album  [r=728278].  

	By the end of the 70s, as [l=Sweet Silence Studios] (owned by former Rosenberg “pupil” Flemming Rasmussen), takes over the leading position and Sonet turns more and more towards pop music, Rosenberg gradually loses interest in his studio. He finally quits by the end of 1978 and the studio becomes [l=Studio 39]. The  deroute is inevitable: Rosenberg, for two decades Danish rock music’s most prominent recording engineer, turns to make his living by copying X-rated movies.

