	Sherman''s father was George Winthrop Fairchild, a co-founder and the first Chairman of [l=IBM]. In 1915, Fairchild enrolled in [l=Harvard University], where he invented the first synchronized shutter and flash for the photo camera. Sherman subsequently transferred to [l=University Of Arizona] for the drier climate due to his health issues but settled in New York, attending [l=Columbia University] for a year. He never graduated and pursued an entrepreneurial career, primarily interested in photography and aeronautics. After his father died in 1924, Sherman Fairchild, as he didn''t have siblings, inherited the entire multimillion-dollar estate, becoming the largest individual IBM stockholder.



	In 1931, Sherman Fairchild started the "Fairchild Recording Equipment Corporation" in Whitestone, New York, mainly producing professional audio electronics. He collaborated with legendary [a=Robert Fine] and Lawrence Scully to develop one of the first variable pitch systems for cutting records. The company''s best-known product was a tube-based mono compressor [b]Fairchild 660[/b] (and its dual-channel version [b]Fairchild 670[/b]), designed by Rein Narma in 1959 for [a=Les Paul]; Fairchild licensed Narma''s design and hired him as the chief engineer. The first three Fairchild 660s were bought by [a=Les Paul & Mary Ford], a New York-based [l=Olmsted Sound Studios], and [a=Rudy Van Gelder], who used it to cut lacquers for [l=Blue Note] and [url=https://discogs.com/label/232007]VOX[/url]. After hearing Fairchild 660 at [url=https://discogs.com/label/281520]Capitol Studios[/url] during his American visit, [a=Peter Bown] from [l=Abbey Road Studios] ordered twelve units. [a=The Beatles] often used them, primarily on vocals, but [a=Geoff Emerick] also added 660s on [a=Ringo Starr]''s drums and some piano and guitar tracks. Other notable producers and engineers who favored Fairchild limiters include [a=Chris Thomas] on [a=Pete Townshend]''s solo albums, [a=Jack Joseph Puig], or [a=Tom Elmhirst] on [a=Amy Winehouse]''s [i]Rehab[/i] and [url=https://www.discogs.com/artist/819015]Adele[/url]''s [i]Hello[/i]. Fairchild 660/670s are still highly desirable due to outstanding tonal characteristics and rareness, fetching prices well above $40,000.



	In the early 1950s, Sherman Fairchild became the [l=Audio Engineering Society] president, revitalizing AES Conventions, suggesting commercial ads in the [i]Journal of the Audio Engineering Society[/i], and making other crucial organizational changes. AES granted Fairchild the Gold Medal in 1955, followed by AES Award in 1961, Fellowship in ''63, and Honorary Membership in 1967. In his modernist New York mansion near Central Park, Sherman Fairchild had a [url=https://www.discogs.com/label/1603808]fully-equipped studio[/url] with two grand pianos, where many jazz musicians had recorded. He was an avid music fan, co-managed a publishing business with [a=Gene Austin], and extensively collaborated with pianist and composer [a=Marian McPartland] and producer, photographer, and ex-CIA agent [a=Hank O''Neal] towards the end of his life. In 1969, they co-founded [l=Halcyon Records] and then a renowned [l=Chiaroscuro Records]. Fairchild died at Roosevelt Hospital in New York at 75 due to acute blood poisoning from the impure batch of intravenous glucose.');

