	At the age of twelve, he first heard Lars Gullin''s famous jazz song "Danny''s dream". At the age of thirteen, he was given his first interview, with Putte Wickman.



	In 1976, he proposed to the record company Caprice that they should release a series of compilation albums of Swedish jazz. The proposal resulted in the work on [l441012], which would continue into the 21st century. The music on the albums ranges from the early 20th century Pinets Orchestra to the newly formed Radio Jazz Group in the late 1960s. The music discs were supplemented with packed booklets containing interviews and photographs. After 2000, Bruér was given the opportunity to work full-time on the project through a scholarship from the Riksbankens Jubileumsfond. The tenth and final album of Svensk Jazzhistoria was released in 2006. Bengt Nyqvist and Jan Bruér had been working on the release for three decades. Together with Kjell Andersson, he published a ten-CD compilation box set with Olle Adolphson''s music.



	In 2010, he was awarded Riksförbundet Svensk Jazz honorary prize Bassisten for his work on the [l441012] compilation box set and his jazz history book publications. In 2013, it was time for a new compilation box set produced in collaboration with Kjell Andersson. Povel Ramel''s debut, released before Christmas, contained six CDs with Povel Ramel''s recordings for Telefunken and Scala. In the same year, Bruér published the biography Putte Wickman: clarinetist, which depicts the jazz musician''s life through extensive interview material with Wickman''s family and acquaintances, including several musicians, as well as Wickman himself through two previously taped interviews. Together with Martin Nyström, Sofia Lilly Jönsson and Gunnel Fagius, Bruér published the biography Georg Riedel Jazzmusiker och kompositör in 2020. In 2021, he published the biography Lars Gullin: saxophonist och kompositör, which built on the work Bruér had begun with his dissertation. The biography is expanded with both archival material and interviews with Gullin''s first and third wives.');



