	John "Candy" Candido played bass and did trick vocal novelties with a voice that spanned four octaves, from a squeaky soprano to a low, growling bass; his normal speaking range was a tenor. 



	Not yet 11 years old, Candido joined his first band in 1924. It was founded by his childhood friend, trumpeter [l=Louis Prima], then 14. Other band members were clarinetist [a=Irving Fazola] and drummer Johnny Viviano.



	Between 1932 and 1944, Candido played bass and occasionally used his vocal talents on recordings of [a=Ted Fiorito & His Orchestra], first on [l=Brunswick], then on [l=Decca]. In 1934, he recorded duets for [l=Vocalion (2)] with guitarist [url=https://www.discogs.com/artist/3679397-Otto-Heimal]Otto "Coco" Heimal[/url] under the name [a=Candy and Coco]. In 1937, he recorded a few titles as "Candy without Coco" for the novelty label [a=Hollywood Hot Shots], probably with other members of the Fiorito orchestra. In the late 1940s, Candido recorded with [a=Jimmy Durante] and on his own for [l=MGM Records]. Starting in November 1946, he weekly appeared on Durante''s radio show where he made the phrase "I''m feelin'' miiiighty low" a national catchphrase. In the early 1950s, he also made a number of children''s records for [l=Capitol Records].



	From the mid-1930s, Candido''s voice talents were frequently featured in the movies. As early as 1934, he and Heimal had a scene as cafe entertainers in the Joan Crawford movie "Sadie McKee". In 1935, he sang a duet with Fred Astaire ("Let''s Begin") in the film "Roberta". He also voiced the angry Apple Tree in the movie "The Wizard of Oz" (1939). He played numerous roles in animated movies, especially for Walt Disney, like the Indian Chief in "Peter Pan" (1953), one of Maleficent''s goons in "Sleeping Beauty" (1959), and the crocodile Captain of the Guard in "Robin Hood" (1973).');
