	Thibodeau received his electronics training in the military in 1977, working mostly on audio equipment. He started his engineering career in 1979 as an assistant engineer at a Connecticut facility. He moved to Nashville in 1981, and joined an up-and-coming country/pop group and traveled across the country. In 1982 he left the group and enrolled at Middle Tennessee State University, and soon became a part-time chief engineer at the Recording Industry Management Department''s recording studio at the university. This led to a full-time position in 1987, and he worked there as the chief engineer for six years. He left MTSU in 1993 and moved back to New England to pursue a career as a freelance technician. 



	He worked at Pro Audio Design from 1998 to 2005. He then returned to freelance work where he branched into broadcast audio doing commentary audio for the Olympics in Beijing and London along with the World Cup in South Africa in 2010. In 2005 he also began designing Pro Audio equipment with [a=Geoff Daking], and he also designed some compressors for Dramastic Audio. In 2012 he accepted a position at Analog Devices as an applications engineer.



	He started his educational career while at MTSU, developing and teaching the Recording Studio Maintenance course. In 1997, he started teaching the Recording Studio Maintenance course at U-Mass, Lowell. He is also an Associate Professor at the department of Music Production and Engineering (MP&E) at Berklee College of Music.');

