	Юрий Алексеевич Балашов (14 сентября 1955, Москва — 19 ноября 2020, там же) — советский, американский и российский художник, музыкант. Обладатель премии «Грэмми» за оформление обложки альбома [m47701] (1994). Один из основателей и участников этно-электронного проекта «[a125618]», а также ряда других музыкальных коллективов.');



	Despite receiving a junior form contract with Celtic football club, John chose to pursue his true passion for music. After completing his apprenticeship as an electrician, he served in the Royal Artillery Band in Dortmund, Germany, during his National Service.



	In the early 1960s, John played with various bands in the Glasgow area, including notable stints with Benny Daniels and Johnny Kildare. His versatility and talent led him to London, where he joined the Ray McVey Orchestra. Here, his career gained momentum as he became the lead trumpet player for acclaimed shows like West Side Story, Billy Liar, and Jesus Christ Superstar. John''s adaptability allowed him to collaborate with a range of artists, including Jack Jones, Aretha Franklin, Paul McCartney, Tom Jones, and more.



	A pivotal moment in his career came in 1970 when he was invited to lead the trumpet section in the big band of Canadian trumpet virtuoso Maynard Ferguson. This collaboration propelled him to greater recognition, with appearances on television, performances at prestigious jazz venues like Ronnie Scott''s, and world tours alongside renowned jazz musicians.



	Despite his success, John Donnelly remained grounded as a family-oriented individual. He returned to Paisley around 1975, where he continued to perform at venues such as the Talk of the Town and BBC Scotland. As opportunities for musicians dwindled, John transitioned to a career in music education, excelling as an instructor of brass instruments. His dedication and passion for teaching earned him high regard among his students and peers.



	John Donnelly possessed an extraordinary musical ear and the ability to arrange compositions without relying on a piano. While his compositions were relatively few, their quality reflected his status as a skilled jazz composer.



	Remembered for his proficiency as both a lead trumpet player and a jazz improviser, John Donnelly''s contributions to British jazz remain significant. His musical legacy continues to inspire and enrich the world of jazz, leaving an enduring mark on the genre.









	');

	Dickerson grew up in St. Louis, where he played in local theatres 1918-1920; toured with Wilson Robinson''s Bostonians in 1923, after which the band took up residency at the Cotton Club in New York under leadership of [a=Andy Preer]. When Preer died in 1927 the band worked as the Cotton Club Orchestra, then as [a=The Missourians], to become [a=Cab Calloway And His Orchestra] in 1930. Dickerson left Calloway in 1931 and ceased to work as a musician.');



	By the time his recording debut, Erotica, was released by Epic Records in 1990, Perera had earned a reputation as one of the pioneers in electro-acoustic harp performance. His five subsequent albums for Heads Up infused the jazz-pop idiom with a wide range of multicultural flavorings, punctuated with guest performances by Trinidadian steel drummer Othello Molineaux, Nicaraguan salsa singer Luis Enrique, Floridian hand percussionist Robert Thomas Jr., and Cuban jazz reedman Paquito D''Rivera.



	Perera was recognized throughout the music industry in 1993 when he won the Billboard Contemporary Latin Jazz Album of the Year award for his second Heads Up release, Dreams & Desires. He was selected as musical director for the Hispanic Heritage awards at the Kennedy Center in 1997 and 1998 was voted Favorite Jazz Artist in his category numerous times in the annual Jazziz magazine reader''s poll. He guested on numerous recordings including D''Rivera''s 100 Years of Latin Love Songs and Gloria Estefan''s Grammy-winning Abriendo Puertas. Perera''s own compositions for In the Mood include the contagious samba "Joia" and the sensual "Six A.M." A totally different approach is taken on "Coming Home," originally penned by Perera in the style of a piece of Paraguayan folk music, but imaginatively adapted to the contemporary jazz idiom for In the Mood.[2] Perera is always looking to add new ideas, to experiment with different styles, and to push the parameters of the instrument while showing that the harp can be very cool and hip.');



	Born: 1920 in Louisville, KY

	Died: February 14, 2004 in Tiburon, CA



	During a career that spanned 50 years Sales wrote about jazz, film and cultural politics and published widely in the San Francisco Chronicle, San Francisco Magazine, the Los Angeles Times, the Tiburon Ark and Gene Lees'' Jazzletter. He wrote three books: Jazz: America''s Classical Music, a biography of John Maher and, with his wife Georgia, The Clay-Pot Cookbook, which sold more than 800,000 copies.



	Sales was also publicist for the Monterey Jazz Festival from its birth in 1958 until 1965, and for the hungry i nightclub. He also did freelance publicity work for artists such as Duke Ellington, Louis Armstrong, Judy Garland and Dick Gregory, and wrote liner notes for several Fantasy recordings.



	Over the years, he taught jazz history courses at Stanford University, the San Francisco Conservatory of Music, San Francisco State University and the JazzSchool.



	Sales became a jazz fan at 16, after hearing a broadcast of Benny Goodman''s band with drummer Gene Krupa, and later became what he called "an inveterate Ellington groupie" after hearing a recording of "Black And Tan Fantasy".



	After serving in the Army Air Corps in Southeast Asia during World War II, Sales studied at Reed College in Portland, Oregon, and then settled in the Bay Area, where he received a BA in history from the University of California at Berkeley. (from San Francisco Chronicle, Feb. 2004)');

