	He studied American Studies at Yale University, graduating in 1961 with an undergraduate thesis on the musicals of the 1920s, and returned to Yale for law school, graduating in 1967. Asked in his final year to organise the papers [a=Cole Porter] had left to the university, he never practised law and instead served as curator of Yale''s collection of the literature of the American musical theatre from 1967 to 1971. He was a music and dance critic for the New York Post and artistic adviser to the Cole Porter and [a=Ira Gershwin] estates. In 1982 he took part in the cataloguing of a warehouse find in Secaucus, New Jersey, which yielded around seventy previously unknown songs by [a=George Gershwin] and more than one hundred and seventy five unpublished songs by [a=Jerome Kern], along with manuscripts and original orchestrations by other Broadway composers and arrangers. With [a=William Bolcom] he wrote Reminiscing With Sissle And Blake (1973), which helped restore attention to the songwriting team of [a=Noble Sissle] and [a=Eubie Blake]. He wrote illustrated biographies of Cole Porter and the Gershwins and edited volumes of the complete lyrics of Porter, [a=Lorenz Hart], Ira Gershwin, [a=Irving Berlin], [a=Frank Loesser] and [a=Johnny Mercer]. He frequently appears as a liner notes author.');



