	He was a longtime member of the National Academy of Recording Arts and Sciences, served on its Board of Governors, and chaired the Album Packaging Committee, which oversaw the Grammy awards recognizing album cover and package design. His design for Fandango’s Last Kiss received a Grammy Award nomination for Best Recording Package in 1979. The album was later featured on the cover of TASCHEN’s Extraordinary Records, a landmark survey of innovative album-cover art, affirming its status as a classic of the form. Throughout his career, his work received numerous honors, including Rolling Stone’s Album Cover of the Year recognition.

	A graduate of the Art Center School of Design in Los Angeles, Bryant began his career as an art director at ABC Records and RCA before becoming creative director of Gribbitt! Studio, where he helped create some of the era’s most distinctive and enduring album imagery. He later founded Bryant & Associates with his wife and longtime collaborator, designer Diane Bryant. The firm, later known as Bryant Design, expanded beyond music into corporate identity, branding, logo design, packaging, and advertising for clients ranging from Dow Chemical to House of Blues.');

