	Elniff began in 1952 - just 14 years old - as a drummer in [a=Ib Glindemann And His Orchestra] which he was associated with for six years. He then studied at [l=Det Kongelige Danske Musikkonservatorium] in Copenhagen. With a mixture of technically refined tonal variation, he became one of the most popular jazz musicians of the time. He played with [a=Oscar Pettiford] and not least with [a=Finn Savery] and [a=Erik Moseholm''s Trio]. In 1961, Jørn Elniff was chosen as Jazz Musician of the Year by [a=Den Danske Jazzkreds] – aged just 23. In the first half of the 1960s, he began to accompany American guest musicians when they visited Copenhagen. He played with i.a. [a=Eric Dolphy], [a=Stan Getz], [a=Brew Moore], [a=Bud Powell], [a=Dexter Gordon], [a=Don Byas] and [a=Lucky Thompson]. In the early 1970s, Elniff often worked with [a=Ole Matthiessen]''s trio and with [a=Duke Jordan]. After that, things went downhill for Jørn Elniff due to alcohol abuse and illness, and in the 1980s he was only rarely heard.');

