	Born in London, Shaw played with Teddy Foster''s band during World War II at the age of 15. In the latter half of the decade he played in London with Oscar Rabin, Frank Weir, and Tommy Sampson, then switched permanently to playing bebop music in 1946 after hearing Dizzy Gillespie. He visited the United States in 1947 with close friend and fellow pioneer bebopper altoist Freddy Syer, then moved to Canada after he was unable to secure a work permit. There they played with Oscar Peterson and Maynard Ferguson before returning to England in 1948. He was one of the early Club Eleven players, along with Ronnie Scott, John Dankworth, Lennie Bush, and others; he also played with many of these musicians on the recordings of Alan Dean''s Beboppers.

	After Club Eleven shuttered, Shaw played with Vic Lewis and toured Europe with Cab Kaye, then joined Jack Parnell''s ensemble in 1953 and Ronnie Scott''s nonet in 1954. Shaw played regularly both live and as a session musician for many British jazz musicians over the course of the next twenty or so years, working with Joe Harriott, Tony Crombie, Don Rendell, Tony Kinsey, Stan Tracey, Bill Le Sage, and others. He led a quartet at the 100 Club in the 1960s, and played in the Bebop Preservation Society and the John Burch Quartet for over two decades each. He retired due to ill health in the late 1990s and died in Kent four months past his 80th birthday. His wife Jennifer of nearly 50 years survived him, dying in August 2007. Hank Shaw was survived by four of his five children; Lester, Howard, Naomi & Russell.



	While Shaw told an interviewer, "I play that way because that''s the way jazz appeals to me." He could have added that many observers saw him as one of this country''s most exciting bebop soloists. Shaw was from the same East End Jewish background as Tony Crombie and Ronnie Scott, but was brought up in an orphanage where "you either joined the band or went in for boxing." He was given a cornet and began to learn to play. At the outbreak of the second world war, Shaw was evacuated and had to relinquish the cornet, but was provided with an instrument when he joined a Salvation Army band, an unusual outlet for a Jewish lad.



	He felt confident enough, on leaving school aged 14, to approach bandleader Jack Jackson, then riding high at the Mayfair Hotel. Jackson took him on, realised that the youngster was a poor sight-reader but allowed him to complete a six-week engagement. Shaw had improved considerably, setting in train his progress through the ranks of the leading dance bands of the day, starting with Teddy Foster, followed in 1945 by Oscar Rabin. This band toured in France, where Shaw encountered top US army musicians like trumpeter Zeke Zarchy, whom, he said, "taught me more in a few days than I''d ever learned before".



	Shaw then set off to the US to hear bebop for himself but, finding work hard to obtain without a permit, moved on to Canada where he jammed with local stars Oscar Peterson and trumpeter Maynard Ferguson.



	Back home in 1948, he became a founder member of Club Eleven, a consortium of like-minded players. Spearheaded by John Dankworth and Ronnie Scott, they made significant recordings (Shaw recorded with Scott and vocalist Alan Dean), attracting considerable attention while playing at the less than salubrious Mack''s Rehearsal Rooms in Great Windmill Street. During this period Shaw also excelled in jazzy big bands led by Vic Lewis and Leon Roy.



	After more years on the dance band circuit and gigs with Jack Parnell and Tony Crombie, Shaw became a key part of Scott''s nine-piece band in 1954, a unit packed with the best British modernists, including Victor Feldman and Phil Seamen. Shaw thought it "a marvellous, roaring band" and stayed on when Scott created his ill-starred larger orchestra, where Shaw was singled out for his endearing habit of sending his poker winnings straight home to his wife.



	He joined Jamaican alto saxophonist Joe Harriott in his celebrated quintet in 1958 but left with pianist Harry South when Harriott sought to introduce his "free-form" concept.



	Shaw became a sought-after section player and soloist on orchestral recordings by Stan Tracey, Kenny Wheeler and Harry South. Perhaps his most rewarding association was cemented when pianist Bill LeSage formed the Bebop Preservation Society in the 1970s, a quintet devoted to straight-ahead modernism in which Shaw flourished alongside altoist Peter King. When ex-Charlie Parker trumpeter Red Rodney performed with the band, bassist Spike Heatley recalled, Shaw was "never overawed and Red respected him". Heatley also said that Shaw took up Pelmanism to sharpen his memory, only to forget where he had put his trumpet, and that "his advanced yoga studies" seemed to be largely an excuse for extra sleep.



	Long since based in Whitstable, Kent, Shaw''s later years were spent leading his own quartet or performing with pianist John Burch''s local octet.



