	In 1937, after performing as a musician with Bangsawan opera troupes, A. Rahman took part in a keroncong singing competition at Happy World amusement park, where he won second place. This led to opportunities to record with EMI Columbia Records, and later with His Master’s Voice (HMV), one of the world’s major recording companies.



	During the Japanese occupation beginning in 1942, A. Rahman frequently performed with his orchestra for Japanese troops. His son, R. Ismail, who was still a child at the time, often accompanied him.



	After the war ended in 1945, A. Rahman formed his own orchestra called Orkes Rahman Kenchanawati. He performed as both a singer and violinist, sometimes doing both simultaneously. The orchestra included well-known singers such as Momo Latiff, Miss Julia, Nona Asiah, and his son R. Ismail. He frequently recorded duet songs with Miss Julia, including Asli Mak Inang and Cik Siti Lela Mayang.



	A. Rahman was a frequent winner of singing competitions and was once honoured with the title “Johan Biduan dari Malaya” (Champion Singer of Malaya). His orchestra performed widely at public events, weddings, radio broadcasts on Radio Malaya, and popular entertainment venues such as Happy World and New World.



	He also received an award from Sultan Ibrahim of Johor for composing the song Sri Johor, which celebrated the uniqueness of the state.



	A. Rahman passed away on 31 October 1957 and was buried at Kaki Bukit Muslim Cemetery in Singapore. He is remembered as a pioneer of Malay music, alongside figures such as Wak Jahari Jarr, Zubir Said, and Ahmad Jaafar, and his contributions remain an important part of Malay musical heritage.');

