	After retirement in May 1997, he went on an international tour of lectures and demonstrations for the United Musical Instrument Company. He visited colleges and high schools and often played as a soloist with the school''s jazz band or symphonic ensemble. In 1998, Modell assembled a group that included nine alumni of the NIU Jazz Ensemble for [a=Phil Collins]'' summer world tour. He also did some stand-up comedy in his later years, appearing regularly at McCurdy''s Comedy Theatre in Sarasota.



	On July 2007, Modell played trumpet for the last time at his 38th consecutive Independence Day concert as soloist with the DeKalb Municipal Band (the musician was 72 y.o. at the time). He wrote a book of memoirs titled [i]Loved Bein'' Here With You[/i], published with a foreword by [a=Quincy Jones].');



	When Modell was assigned to create a jazz program at the University from scratch, he already had some experience: before joining the NIU, he successfully established a stage band at [l=Southern Methodist University] in Texas. NIU Jazz Ensemble played the first concert in October 1969, only six weeks after the launch. Under the talented leadership and with a strong cast of musicians, the orchestra quickly rose to the national fame. In the summer of 1977, NIU Jazz Ensemble joined Dizzy Gillespie as the featured performers at the annual conference of the International Association of Jazz Educators (IAJE) at Dallas.



	In 1983, PBS aired a documentary called [i]A Year in the Life of the Greatest College Jazz Band in America[/i] about the orchestra, which won two Chicago Emmy awards. NIU Jazz Ensemble traveled to Europe that summer and appeared at the famous Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland. The live recording of that concert was [url=https://www.discogs.com/release/4537059]released[/url] in 1984. The ensemble performed at the 54th annual Midwest Music Clinic in Chicago with saxophonist [a=Jimmy Heath] as the guest artist, and at the 28th annual IAJE Conference in New York with trumpeter [a=Wynton Marsalis]. In the summer of 1996, the NIU Jazz Orchestra returned to the Montreux Jazz Festival as [a=Quincy Jones] big band with guest artists [a=Phil Collins], [a=Patti Austin], [a=David Sanborn], [a=Gerald Albright], [a=Chaka Kahn], and [a=James Morrison].



	During the summer of 2001, the band performed at the North Sea Jazz Festival in the Netherlands, Jazz à Vienne Festival in France, and Montreux Jazz Festival in Switzerland.');

