	His adventure with photography began in the 1970s, when as a high school student he experimented with special techniques such as isohelia, collage, and coloring of monochromatic prints. During this time, he began collaborating with the [l46867], for which he created covers and documented concerts. In the 1970s, he was also the author of many promotional photos for the [l718035] and the [l732622]. From 1981, he was a member of the [url=https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Society_of_Authors_ZAiKS]ZAiKS Authors'' Association[/url].



	During the period of martial law in Poland, his longing for color led him to create a unique technique: painting on black-and-white photographs using transparent Ecoline inks. With this method, he created the cover for the first edition of Tomasz Stańko''s album, [m513718] (1985).



	From 1987 to 1990, Kłosek served as head of the photography department at the [l34482]. During this period, he took photos for the covers of albums by such prominent composers and artists as [a853983], [a227875], and [a1012358]. He also collaborated on creating covers for jazz and popular music artists, such as [m2502568].



	From 1989, he worked with the [url=https://www.discogs.com/label/70677-Kompania-Muzyczna-Pomaton?redirected=true]Pomaton[/url] record label, for which he designed and created the album covers for [m2280292], [m677772], and [m1833440]. In 1993, he used the photographic collage technique to create the famous cover [m583005].



	In later years, Piotr Kłosek focused on advertising photography, press photojournalism, and album photography.');

