	Sandy Warner went down in history as The Face of the Exotica genre after her collaboration as a cover star model for [a36860]''s albums. She made her debut as the cover girl on his 1957 album [m=172407] and then appeared on the album''s first 11 sequels and some of their corresponding singles. 
	She went on to release an album of her own in 1959: [m=542433], for which Martin Denny wrote the sleeve notes. She also appeared as cover girl for other artists.

	Her role as a cover star or model for the artwork of those albums (except her solo discs) was never mentioned anywhere in the credits on the covers of such releases. Which was a common practice in the past due to contractual reasons, possibly. Almost a sole exemption from that rule was an album from 1959 by [m172401]. There she is given credit for her work as a cover star for an album''s front cover image shoot by the creative team of Garrett-Howard, who designed quite a few Exotica albums and many similar others from the era. This credit could be found on the back of the sleeve, and that reads, ''Cover Posed By Sandy Warner, "The Exotica Girl".''

	Due to that fact and in accordance with Discogs standards, it would be technically incorrect to list releases starring Sandy Warner as a cover star on them in her name on her Discogs artist''s profile. Such entries may be voted to be removed by moderators. 

	Instead, in order to do justice to Sandy Warner – The Face of the Exotica genre and also assist potential new audiences in finding out more music in indirect ways (as collecting albums by cover stars etc.) yet still remain technically correct with Discogs standards, – it will be sufficient to add links to Master Releases bearing Sandy Warner as a model on the covers here among the notes of her artist''s profile.

