	Although there is no recorded traces, Phil Raphael also played in [a=Tom Dorsey] and [a=Stan Kenton]’s big bands and then moved to Las Vegas for a while. Raphaël appeared under unknown circumstances in Belgium in the 1960s, playing among others at the 1966 Jazz Bilzen festival, and he eventually settled in Brussels. A multifaceted musician, he did not limit himself to jazz and also worked in pop groups, directed the music for the spectacle Hair, and even had a brief residency at Pol’s Jazz Club where he played the music of [a=Johann Sebastian Bach] four nights per week. 

	Raphaël’s passage through the Belgian nightlife was just as mysterious as his music, and few people seem to remember him. Drummer [a=Bruno Castellucci] describes him as remarkable, both as a musician and as a person: “He was a hippie before there were hippies. He wasn’t part of the system but he had a system of his own.”');

