	Born: January 18, 1932, Johannesburg, South Africa

	Died: January 1, 2024, South Africa



	Peter Magubane, born into the vibrant but oppressed world of Johannesburg in 1932, became one of South Africa''s most distinguished photojournalists, using his lens as a weapon against apartheid. Growing up in Sophiatown, a melting pot of cultures that would later be razed in the name of segregation, Magubane was gifted a Kodak Box Brownie by his father, sparking a lifelong passion for photography. Despite the racial divisions and personal hardships he faced, including a restrictive upbringing under apartheid''s draconian laws, Magubane''s early fascination with photography led him to a career that would document some of the most pivotal moments in South Africa''s history.



	Magubane''s professional journey began earnestly with Drum magazine, a publication known for its critical stance on apartheid and its vibrant portrayal of black urban life. His work with Drum and later The Rand Daily Mail put him at the forefront of photojournalism during a period of intense political and social upheaval in South Africa. Magubane''s daring and innovative techniques, such as hiding his camera in loaves of bread or hollowed-out Bibles to take clandestine photographs, allowed him to capture images that others couldn''t. His commitment to exposing the brutal reality of apartheid, despite facing banning orders, solitary confinement, and beatings, underscored his bravery and dedication to the truth.



	Magubane''s photography during the Soweto uprising and other significant anti-apartheid protests not only brought international attention to South Africa''s struggle but also made him a target for the apartheid regime. His ability to capture the raw emotion and stark realities of the era, from the Sharpeville massacre to the trials and tribulations of Nelson Mandela and the ANC, earned him global acclaim and numerous awards. Yet, it was his photograph of a black maid attending to a white child, a poignant symbol of apartheid''s racial divide, that Magubane regarded as one of his most powerful works.



	Throughout his career, Magubane never lost sight of his mission to fight apartheid through his photography. He was a silent observer whose images spoke volumes, capturing the dignity, suffering, and resilience of his subjects. His work played a crucial role in drawing international condemnation against the apartheid regime and remains a testament to the power of photojournalism in advocating for justice and change.



	In his later years, Magubane''s focus shifted towards capturing the beauty of South Africa''s landscapes and people, moving away from the violence and pain that had characterized much of his earlier work. However, his legacy as a fearless chronicler of South Africa''s journey from apartheid to democracy continues to inspire future generations.');



