	He is the founder of the Tomasz Chyła Quintet, with which he has released five albums: Eternal Entropy (2017) (nomination by the Polish Phonographic Academy for the Fryderyk Music Award 2017 in the Jazz Music category; nomination for Phonographic Debut of the Year), Circlesongs (2018) (nomination by the Polish Phonographic Academy for the Fryderyk Music Award 2018 in the Jazz Music category for Best Jazz Album of the Year), Tomasz Chyła Quintet – Live at Polish Radio 3 (2019), da Vinci (2021), and Music We Like To Dance To (2024) (nomination by the Polish Phonographic Academy for the Fryderyk Music Award 2024 in the Jazz Music category for Best Jazz Album of the Year). The quintet has performed at festivals across Europe and Asia.

	He is a member of ensembles such as Jazz Forum Talents and Carillion Electric Orchestra. In 2022, he founded the band HÉR, which goes beyond the jazz framework, musically touching on heavier sounds, as evidenced by signing a contract with the French label Season of Mist. The band’s debut album will be released in 2026.

	Individually, he was nominated for the Fryderyk Phonographic Award in 2019 and 2024 in the Jazz Music category as Jazz Artist of the Year. In 2020, he received the Grand Prix Jazz Melomani Award in the “Jazz Hope” category. He has been repeatedly ranked at the very top of the annual Jazz Top poll by the prestigious Jazz Forum magazine. He is a multiple scholarship recipient, including from the Ministry of Culture, the Marshal of the Pomeranian Voivodeship, and the Mayor of the City of Gdańsk. In 2018, he received a double Award of the City of Gdańsk for Young Creators in the Field of Culture.

	In addition to his activity as an instrumentalist, he has achieved significant success in the field of choral music. In 2011, he co-founded the vocal ensemble Art’n’Voices, with which he won top prizes at national and international competitions and festivals. Together with the ensemble, he has recorded seven albums: three original releases and four collaborative ones. For their latest release, Midnight Stories, the ensemble received the Polish Phonographic Academy’s Fryderyk Award 2021 in the “Contemporary Music” category. The ensemble’s next album, Pieces of Myself, will be released in October 2025.

	Since 2015, he has been the conductor of the Women’s Chamber Choir at the J. Wybicki First High School in Kościerzyna, with which he participates in the nationwide project Akademia Chóralna – Śpiewająca Polska (Choral Academy – Singing Poland). The choir has won numerous awards at regional and national competitions.

	From 2015 to 2024, he led the mixed choir Cantores Veiherovienses, winning accolades at many national and international festivals.

	As a conductor, Tomasz Chyła has repeatedly been honored with awards at choral competitions as Best Conductor of the Competition, including at the National Festival of Songs of the Sea in Wejherowo, the Pomeranian Passion Song Festival in Szemud, and several times at the regional competitions of Singing Poland.

	He is the originator and artistic director of the National Festival of Sacred Song “Pater Noster,” held in his hometown of Strzepcz. To date, six editions of the festival have taken place. Strong ties with the municipality of Linia resulted in the publication of a book about the activities of the Pięciolinia Choir from Linia, released in 2017. He is currently the Vice President of the Association of Young Artists of the Wejherowo Region and of the Art of Freedom Foundation.

	Since 2024, he has served as the Artistic Director of the Gdańsk-based music festival Art of Freedom Jazz Festival.

